#
# BEFORE PUBLISHING — two things here must be decided by a human, not by the
# tooling:
#
# 1. THE AUTHOR LIST. The entry below is the named author, confirmed by the
#    project owner. A Zenodo DOI is permanent and public, and it cannot be
#    quietly reassigned later — being named on it is a public, citable
#    attribution, so anyone added later must agree to be named.
#
# 2. THE DOI. Zenodo mints it on the first upload. Put the CONCEPT DOI here (the
#    one covering all versions), not the version DOI: the concept DOI always
#    resolves to the latest release. No DOI is listed below yet, because
#    inventing a placeholder identifier risks it being copied into a real
#    citation. Add it after the first successful Zenodo upload.
#
# The ORCID below is an all-zero placeholder, which is the conventional
# "no ORCID" sentinel. Replace it with a real one, or delete the field — do
# not leave a fabricated identifier in place.
#
# The repository URL is the intended public location. Confirm it resolves
# before tagging a release — the sdist check and the Zenodo record depend on it.
#
# The abstract deliberately states the negative results. If you shorten it to a
# positive summary, it will misrepresent the work.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "Quantum Anchor V1.2 — Tesseract Anchor Protocol and Measurement Ledger"
abstract: >-
  An experimental protocol for coherent state anchoring on superconducting QPUs,
  together with the measurement ledger that tests it. The original pulse-level
  protocol (qiskit.pulse, meas_level=0) was deprecated by IBM in 2024 and removed
  from production QPUs in February 2025. V1.2 migrates to working APIs and reports
  evidence grades honestly. Measured: single-qubit dynamics psi(37ns)=0.331662
  under a gamma=0 model on ibm_marrakesh; four independent Bell pairs at 95-96%
  per-plane balance on IQM Resonance Garnet 19Q. Documented negative results: the
  anchor-on and anchor-off conditions both yield 0% clear, and the global 8-qubit
  correlation is 11.62%, consistent with statistically independent planes. The
  physical anchor-drive compensation effect is NOT proven by any measurement in
  this work. An earlier positive result is retracted as a circuit tautology.
